  "background": "Revelation identifies its human author simply as John, a servant and prophetic witness exiled on Patmos, and addresses seven real churches in the Roman province of Asia. Christian tradition often identified this John with John the apostle, while many modern scholars distinguish the seer of Patmos from the Gospel’s author; the text itself does not explicitly say “John the apostle.” The date is also debated. A setting late in the first century under Domitian is common, while some argue for an earlier Neronian setting. What is clear is that the churches lived within Roman imperial power and faced pressures involving worship, commerce, social belonging, compromise, and faithful allegiance to Jesus.\n\nRevelation’s symbolic world is saturated with Israel’s Scriptures—especially Exodus, Psalms, Isaiah, Ezekiel, Daniel, Zechariah, and prophetic oracles against empires. Symbols often work by echo and combination rather than by one-to-one decoding. The visions should therefore be read first through their biblical and first-century setting before modern application. Interpretive traditions differ on chronology, millennium, tribulation, Israel, and fulfillment; those differences should be described honestly without ridicule or treated as tests of Christian faithfulness.",
