      "context": "Revelation 19 answers Babylon’s fall with worship, a marriage celebration, and the appearance of the rider called Faithful and True. His imagery draws from Psalms, Isaiah, and royal-messianic traditions. His robe is already blood-marked before the battle description; interpreters debate whether this primarily evokes His sacrificial blood, Isaiah 63’s judgment imagery, or both. The clearest point is that final judgment belongs to Christ, not to the church.\n\nRevelation 20 has produced several major millennial traditions. Premillennial readings place Christ’s return before a future thousand-year reign; amillennial readings commonly understand the thousand years symbolically as the present reign of Christ and His saints; postmillennial readings expect gospel-shaped historical transformation before Christ’s final return. Variations exist within each. The chapter’s shared center is more secure than any timeline: Satan is limited, faithful witnesses belong to Christ, rebellion is temporary, judgment is real, and death and Hades are finally defeated.",
